WEBVTT

00:00:01.50 --> 00:00:06.76
This is journey twenty four of Hewn, the software factory: manage worker tokens.

00:00:06.76 --> 00:00:09.33
A worker proves who it is with a token.

00:00:09.33 --> 00:00:13.67
This video shows an Owner creating one, a worker starting with it,

00:00:13.67 --> 00:00:19.07
and what happens when the Owner revokes it while that worker is in the middle of a

00:00:19.07 --> 00:00:19.33
run.

00:00:20.48 --> 00:00:25.79
An Owner creates a worker token, and sees it exactly once; Hewn stores only its

00:00:25.79 --> 00:00:26.12
hash.

00:00:26.12 --> 00:00:31.02
The Owner chooses when it expires: in thirty days, ninety days or a year,

00:00:31.02 --> 00:00:32.77
and it stops working then.

00:00:32.77 --> 00:00:36.79
And when an Owner revokes a token whose worker is connected,

00:00:36.79 --> 00:00:41.63
the worker is disconnected within ten seconds and its lease is released.

00:00:42.78 --> 00:00:45.37
Here is Workers, signed in as an Owner.

00:00:45.37 --> 00:00:50.97
New worker token asks for a name, where the worker runs, and when the token expires.

00:00:50.97 --> 00:00:54.84
The name is shown on Workers and will be in the audit log,

00:00:54.84 --> 00:00:59.50
so it is checked: three to forty lowercase letters, digits or hyphens.

00:00:59.85 --> 00:01:05.57
Create token shows it once, with Copy, and the command that starts a worker with it.

00:01:05.57 --> 00:01:07.61
Copy puts it on the clipboard.

00:01:07.61 --> 00:01:13.18
Once the token is showing, Escape and a click outside do nothing; only Done closes

00:01:13.18 --> 00:01:16.45
the dialog, so the token isn't lost by accident.

00:01:17.60 --> 00:01:19.65
Here is how a token is made.

00:01:19.65 --> 00:01:25.73
It is the prefix h w t underscore, then thirty two random bytes in base sixty four.

00:01:25.73 --> 00:01:29.91
The prefix lets secret scanners recognise one that leaks.

00:01:30.26 --> 00:01:35.51
Creating a token stores its SHA two fifty six, with the name, location,

00:01:35.51 --> 00:01:40.76
who created it and the expiry, and answers with the token itself, once,

00:01:40.76 --> 00:01:41.95
marked no store.

00:01:42.30 --> 00:01:48.31
This is the row Hewn keeps for the token just created: an id, the name, a hash,

00:01:48.31 --> 00:01:49.45
and the expiry.

00:01:49.45 --> 00:01:51.13
The token isn't in it.

00:01:51.13 --> 00:01:54.10
Nor is it in any response from Workers.

00:01:54.45 --> 00:01:57.58
Every worker request is checked by hash.

00:01:57.58 --> 00:02:02.52
A token that is unknown, revoked or past its expiry gets a 401.

00:02:02.52 --> 00:02:06.99
The token set in the API's own configuration still works,

00:02:06.99 --> 00:02:11.30
as a development token for a local stack and the tests.

00:02:12.45 --> 00:02:14.67
Here the command runs with Docker.

00:02:14.67 --> 00:02:20.10
The worker registers under the token's name and location, whatever it calls itself.

00:02:20.10 --> 00:02:23.83
Then a direction is submitted, and this worker leases it.

00:02:23.83 --> 00:02:26.64
Its row shows the project it is working on,

00:02:26.64 --> 00:02:30.31
and its heartbeat keeps refreshing while the stage runs.

00:02:30.66 --> 00:02:35.67
In the register handler, an issued token supplies the name and the location,

00:02:35.67 --> 00:02:38.18
so Workers shows what the Owner chose.

00:02:38.18 --> 00:02:41.34
The development token leaves both to the worker.

00:02:42.49 --> 00:02:44.80
Each worker row now has Revoke.

00:02:44.80 --> 00:02:49.50
A Viewer sees Workers without it, and without New worker token,

00:02:49.50 --> 00:02:53.07
and the API answers a Viewer's request with 403.

00:02:53.42 --> 00:02:54.64
Revoke asks first.

00:02:54.64 --> 00:02:59.96
The dialog names the worker, and says what happens to a step it is running: the

00:02:59.96 --> 00:03:05.48
lease is released, and the run resumes on another worker from its last checkpoint.

00:03:05.48 --> 00:03:09.05
Confirming revokes the token, and every worker on it,

00:03:09.05 --> 00:03:14.43
and saves that before anything else, so no request on the token is accepted from

00:03:14.43 --> 00:03:14.97
then on.

00:03:15.32 --> 00:03:19.18
Then each run those workers lease is halted and released,

00:03:19.18 --> 00:03:24.79
with a line in its log saying whose token was revoked and the step it resumes from.

00:03:24.79 --> 00:03:28.31
The held control poll is woken, and Workers is told.

00:03:28.66 --> 00:03:31.85
Here it is with the worker in the middle of a step.

00:03:31.85 --> 00:03:34.66
Revoke, confirm, and the row is gone at once.

00:03:34.66 --> 00:03:37.59
The container exits within a third of a second,

00:03:37.59 --> 00:03:41.34
and the standby worker picks the run up from its checkpoint.

00:03:42.49 --> 00:03:46.20
On the worker, any 401 means its token was refused.

00:03:46.20 --> 00:03:49.92
The control poll is held open while a run executes,

00:03:49.92 --> 00:03:53.64
so it hears first: the step in progress is aborted.

00:03:53.64 --> 00:03:57.87
Then the loop ends, and the process exits with code three,

00:03:57.87 --> 00:04:01.88
so a restart policy doesn't keep retrying a dead token.

00:04:02.23 --> 00:04:07.72
The sequence puts it together, with no hub involved: the control poll is how a

00:04:07.72 --> 00:04:10.25
revocation reaches a running worker.

00:04:10.25 --> 00:04:12.30
A d r twenty six records why.

00:04:13.45 --> 00:04:18.30
On the backend, eleven new tests cover creating, expiring and revoking.

00:04:18.30 --> 00:04:21.17
This one revokes a worker holding a lease,

00:04:21.17 --> 00:04:25.07
and checks the held control poll ends inside ten seconds,

00:04:25.07 --> 00:04:29.45
every later call is refused, and another worker resumes the run.

00:04:29.45 --> 00:04:33.21
They failed first, as there was no worker tokens table.

00:04:33.21 --> 00:04:37.38
The backend has two hundred and ninety four tests, all green.

00:04:37.73 --> 00:04:43.39
On the worker, two new tests check that a refused token aborts the run and stops the

00:04:43.39 --> 00:04:43.72
loop.

00:04:43.72 --> 00:04:47.15
They failed first, as the fake API couldn't revoke.

00:04:47.15 --> 00:04:50.38
The worker has one hundred and fifty five tests.

00:04:50.73 --> 00:04:55.89
End to end, four new tests create and copy a token, revoke a leased worker,

00:04:55.89 --> 00:04:58.71
reject a bad name, and keep a Viewer out.

00:04:58.71 --> 00:05:03.38
The dialogs match their mocks, and have no accessibility violations.

00:05:04.53 --> 00:05:07.78
Workers reads the same in dark mode, and on a phone.

00:05:08.93 --> 00:05:11.16
Journey twenty four is complete.

00:05:11.16 --> 00:05:16.17
An Owner creates a worker token that is shown once and stored as a hash,

00:05:16.17 --> 00:05:18.05
with the expiry they chose.

00:05:18.05 --> 00:05:23.56
Revoking it stops its worker within ten seconds, and the run resumes elsewhere.

00:05:23.56 --> 00:05:25.72
Next comes journey twenty five.
